#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e30f645-54b0-5eae-b57e-34f9c4ffffff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfbbf58-a0ba-5f08-ad19-4b0db32db4a1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eee1d4ba-d69b-5847-a859-94521b94f113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eee1d4ba-d69b-5847-a859-94521b94f113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261367} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22aac6e-74b5-5d2e-aa51-0c181d3d7ce6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71771640-36aa-5365-9496-11f66f198e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72064" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a464cdf4-38d9-50a8-8df7-ec69e984734d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a464cdf4-38d9-50a8-8df7-ec69e984734d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261548} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec393cc-fbfd-5b39-83c2-d0012450e168" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41763944-ffc1-5cbe-ae34-8d6954078ad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74505" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA\\approx f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219ce7e5-8d95-57ca-a29e-117e34fb5649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219ce7e5-8d95-57ca-a29e-117e34fb5649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261601} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ca1f87-1e1d-58c4-8107-110f2c30036e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7212979-12af-55e0-b574-fe7390124c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75961" :question/problem "Which value of $y$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators ${y}^{2}$ and $2y.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({y}^{2},2y)=2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =y\\cdot 2y\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) & =y\\cdot \\cancel{2y}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2y}})+2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1) \\\\ 2y+2 & =7y-2 \\\\ 7y-2y & =2+2 \\\\ 5y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}]}
